 "schema": 4,
 "question": "B. Is this measure already running in South Ostrobothnia wellbeing services county (or in the Tays university hospital area that treats its patients)?\nA. Does recent evidence support the size of effect we assume: does this measure shorten the time from first symptom to treatment start for lung cancer patients, and by how many days?\nMeasure: Standardised lung cancer pathway with booked slots. A lung cancer pathway on the Danish/Swedish/Norwegian model: fixed referral criteria, pre-booked CT, PET-CT, bronchoscopy and MDT slots, a named pathway owner, and published time limits for each step.",
 "area_id": "16",
 "verdict": "mixed",
 "effect_check": "weaker",
 "effect_summary_en": "A Swedish national study (Scientific Reports 2023) gives the lung cancer pathway a lead time of 30–44 days, but only 30 % of patients started treatment within it, and it reports no days saved.",
 "supported_en": "Sweden's lung cancer pathway has a lead time of 30–44 days, and 30 % of patients met it (Scientific Reports 2023).",
 "not_supported_en": "None of the pages read shows how many days a pathway saves between first symptom and treatment, so the assumed 14 days (range 5–23) is not confirmed.",
 "in_use_here": "no_evidence",
 "in_use_scope": "none",
 "in_use_quote": null,
 "in_use_source": null,
 "in_use_summary_en": "After three search rounds I found no public sign of a standardised lung cancer pathway with pre-booked slots, a named pathway owner or published time limits for each step at Hyvaep or Tays. Pirha's pathway page gives only typical waiting times.",
 "missing_en": "",
 "summary_en": "Hyvaep's lung clinic page says the clinic investigates suspected lung cancer at Seinäjoki Central Hospital and that lung cancer found there is treated elsewhere case by case. It does not describe a standardised pathway with booked slots or time limits. Pirha's (Tays) pathway page only gives typical times, such as the CT scan usually being done within a week of handling the referral, and does not say whether it covers South Ostrobothnia's patients. The Swedish study shows a lead time of 30–44 days that only 30 % of lung pathway patients met, and it gives no number of days saved.",
 "unanswered_en": "I found no decision, project or published time limits for a standardised lung cancer pathway from Hyvaep or Tays, and no study reporting how many days such a pathway saves.",
